1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,304
Six months in space. Today, gravity
comes back.

2
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:21,108
After microgravity, the inner ear — the
body’s balance sensor — is miscalibrated.

3
00:00:21,148 --> 00:00:24,296
The ground is still. It only feels tilted.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,507
And help is not close.

5
00:00:31,547 --> 00:00:35,937
A message to Earth can take up to
twenty-two minutes — one way.

6
00:00:35,977 --> 00:00:38,960
So we built the instrument for
that moment.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:49,688
We are six students from Bangladesh — one
team, one obsession.

8
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,504
Turning an invisible medical risk into
something an astronaut can actually see.

9
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:17,917
We didn’t want to fake a
spaceship dashboard.

10
00:01:17,957 --> 00:01:21,992
We wanted to answer one honest question —
am I still me today?

11
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,211
Every claim is backed by a test and
a source —

12
00:01:33,251 --> 00:01:37,169
ninety-three automated checks,
fifteen cited references.

13
00:01:37,209 --> 00:01:40,208
If we couldn’t prove it, we didn’t
ship it.

14
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,509
Go back to that moment. If that astronaut
had their own baseline —

15
00:01:49,549 --> 00:01:54,059
their own normal, captured before the
mission — the story changes.

16
00:01:54,099 --> 00:01:59,504
We don’t compare them to an average human.
We compare them to yesterday’s self.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:01,160
Six domains —

18
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:06,984
balance, reaction, symptoms, head motion,
eye-head coordination, drift —

19
00:02:07,024 --> 00:02:11,271
fold into one score: the
Orientation Stability Index.

20
00:02:11,311 --> 00:02:16,784
Beside every score sits its uncertainty,
and a noise floor called MDC.

21
00:02:17,074 --> 00:02:18,893
Every number is traceable.

22
00:02:18,933 --> 00:02:23,398
The science is keyed to NASA’s Human
Research Program, the NIH,

23
00:02:23,438 --> 00:02:27,402
and peer-reviewed literature — cited on
the page itself.

24
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:32,752
And here is what makes us different.

25
00:02:32,792 --> 00:02:37,637
When a camera cannot measure the eyes, we
do not invent a zero.

26
00:02:37,677 --> 00:02:41,592
We write “unavailable” — and widen
the uncertainty.

27
00:02:41,632 --> 00:02:44,384
An honest gap beats a confident lie.

28
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:51,008
This is not a mockup. It runs right now,
in a browser, fully offline.

29
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:02,237
Calibrate in about two seconds.

30
00:03:02,277 --> 00:03:07,232
The camera measures head motion on the
device — nothing is uploaded.

31
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:20,968
Capture a baseline, and the console gives
you a score, its confidence, an advisory,

32
00:03:21,008 --> 00:03:23,284
a countermeasure, and a recheck.

33
00:03:23,324 --> 00:03:28,568
Data moves as a small, delay-tolerant
file — no video streaming required.

34
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:33,383
The decision engine is finished
and deployable today.

35
00:03:33,423 --> 00:03:37,839
What comes next needs hardware:
eye-tracking to fill that honest gap,

36
00:03:37,879 --> 00:03:41,584
force plates and IMUs through our
open ingestion contract,

37
00:03:41,624 --> 00:03:45,846
a crew-wide view for the medical officer,
and clinical validation.

38
00:03:45,886 --> 00:03:49,592
The software is ready — the sensors are
the mission ahead.

39
00:03:49,882 --> 00:03:55,386
VESTIBULAR GPS. We measure what we can —
and we are honest about the rest.

